<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d6" for="edge" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="edge" attr.name="keywords" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="edge" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d3" for="edge" attr.name="weight" attr.type="double" />
  <key id="d2" for="node" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="node" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="node" attr.name="entity_type" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;MARRIED WOMEN'S PROPERTY ACT 1870&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Married Women's Property Act 1870 was an Act of Parliament of the United Kingdom that allowed married women to be the legal owners of the money they earned and to inherit property."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARRIED WOMEN'S PROPERTY ACT 1882&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Married Women's Property Act 1882 was an Act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom that significantly altered English law regarding the property rights of married women, allowing them to own and control property in their own right."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PARLIAMENT OF THE UNITED KINGDOM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Parliament of the United Kingdom is the legislative body that passed the Married Women's Property Acts."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UNITED KINGDOM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The United Kingdom is the country where the Married Women's Property Acts were enacted."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NEW YORK&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"New York granted a married woman the right to hold a patent and retain earnings from it as if unmarried."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ERNESTINE ROSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ernestine Rose campaigned for the Married Women's Property Act since 1836."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PAULINA WRIGHT DAVIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Paulina Wright Davis joined the campaign for the Married Women's Property Act."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELIZABETH CADY STANTON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Elizabeth Cady Stanton was part of the campaign for the Married Women's Property Act."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMMON LAW TRADITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Common Law Traditions refer to the traditional legal systems before the codification of laws."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CODIFICATION OF LAW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Codification of Law refers to the process of creating written laws to replace traditional common law practices."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAMPAIGN FOR WOMEN'S RIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A long and energetic campaign by women's groups and some men led to the passing of the Married Women's Property Acts."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WOMEN'S GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Women's groups played a crucial role in the campaign for the Married Women's Property Acts."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VICTORIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Victoria passed legislation in 1884, similar to the Married Women's Property Act."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NEW SOUTH WALES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"New South Wales passed legislation in 1889, similar to the Married Women's Property Act."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REMAINING AUSTRALIAN TERRITORIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The remaining Australian territories followed suit with similar legislation after the Married Women's Property Acts."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROPERTY RIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Property Rights refer to the legal rights of individuals to own and control property."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INHERITANCE RIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Inheritance Rights refer to the legal rights of individuals to inherit property."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wages refer to the money earned through work."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INVESTMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Investment refers to the financial gain from investments."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GIFT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gift refers to the transfer of property without consideration."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INVENTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Invention refers to a new idea or creation that can be patented."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PATENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Patent refers to a legal document granting exclusive rights to an inventor."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEGISLATIVE BODY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Legislative Body refers to the body responsible for enacting laws."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEGAL REFORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Legal Reform refers to the process of changing existing laws to improve fairness and efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CODIFIED LAW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Codified Law refers to written laws that replace traditional common law practices."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMMON LAW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Common Law refers to the traditional legal system before codification."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAMPAIGN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A long and energetic campaign by women's groups and some men led to the passing of the Married Women's Property Acts."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0343-ff839d84606e</data>
    </node>
